<section class="article-hero"><div class="container"><span class="eyebrow">Student budget</span><h1>Pay-As-You-Go vs Monthly Transcription Plans</h1><p>If your transcription needs change week by week, a monthly plan may not be the best fit. Here is how to compare the real cost.</p><div class="article-meta"><span>Updated May 10, 2026</span><span>5 minute read</span></div></div></section>
<section class="article-layout"><div class="container article-shell">
<article class="article-body">
<p>Students often need transcription in bursts: a lecture series, exam review week, a research interview, or a foreign-language course. Paying every month for software you only use occasionally can become expensive.</p>
<h2>When subscriptions make sense</h2>
<p>A monthly transcription plan may be reasonable if you transcribe many hours every week, need team administration, or use meeting archives daily.</p>
<h2>When pay-as-you-go works better</h2>
<ul><li>You only transcribe during busy academic periods.</li><li>You need lecture notes for a few classes, not every day.</li><li>You want no auto-renewal and no long-term commitment.</li><li>You prefer transparent minute-based usage.</li></ul>
<table class="comparison-table"><tr><th>Model</th><th>Best fit</th><th>Risk</th></tr><tr><td>Monthly subscription</td><td>Heavy weekly usage</td><td>You pay even in quiet months.</td></tr><tr><td>Pay-as-you-go credits</td><td>Irregular study and research needs</td><td>You need to manage remaining minutes.</td></tr></table>
<h2>Audicap's credit model</h2>
<p>Audicap uses a simple model: 1 credit equals 1 minute of transcription. New users get 30 free minutes, and credits expiring soonest are used first. Live translation is included during live sessions.</p>
